# L §5.9 Rows replaced: "Otherwise its `replace` rows must form a whole table (ordered, without gaps or overlaps),
# which replaces the earlier table wholly on the version's span, with its own intervals."  v2's rows [0, 20] and
# (20, inf) are not v1's intervals and form a whole table: in 2027 b(15) is 5 (v1 would give 2) and b(50) is 6; in
# 2026 v1's table gives 2 and 2.  q3: a single replace row (20, inf) is a whole table too, so it replaces v3's table
# wholly and b2(5), outside every row, is a hole ("An x outside every row is a hole").
=== m.tally
model t
  tally 2
  on 2027-01-01
  start 2026-01-01
  end 2027-12-31

source s "s"
band b(n: int): int
band b2(n: int): int
input mid: int
input hi: int
input lo: int

version v1
  cite s
  governs 2026..
  known 2025-12-01
  b: [0, 10] = 1
  b: (10, inf) = 2
  b2: [0, 10] = 1
  b2: (10, inf) = 2
  mid = 15
  hi = 50
  lo = 5

version v2
  cite s
  governs 2027..
  known 2026-12-01
  replace b: [0, 20] = 5
  replace b: (20, inf) = 6
  replace b2: (20, inf) = 3

ask q1: b(mid), b(hi) law 2027-06-30
ask q2: b(mid), b(hi) law 2026-06-30
ask q3: b2(lo) law 2027-06-30
=== expect
q1: (5, 6)
...
q2: (2, 2)
  law date 2026-06-30, cut 2027-01-01
q3: ...
...
